{
  "accessed_at": "2026-11-08T05:21:49.738Z",
  "result": "Genome-scale reconstruction and in silico analysis of the Ralstonia eutropha H16 for polyhydroxyalkanoate synthesis, lithoautotrophic growth, and 2-methyl citric acid production | BMC Systems Biology | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-5-101)\nciteturn47view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-5-101\",\"pattern\":\"Fermentation\"}); Total lines: 498\nL76: eutropha H16 can play an important role in microbial degradation of aromatic compounds such as benzoate, phenol and biphenyl in our environment [【30†7】].\nL77: In order to improve the industrial applicability of R. eutropha H16 by metabolic engineering, it is necessary to understand the characteristics of its metabolism and optimize the entire metabolic network at the systems level. Having the complete genome sequence and annotation of R. eutropha H16 available, genome-scale modeling and simulation can be employed for such systematic analyses and design of metabolic engineering strategies.\nL78: Here we report the reconstruction of the in silico genome-scale model of R. eutropha H16, RehMBEL1391, composed of 1391 reactions including 229 transport reactions and 1171 metabolites (Additional file 【31†1】 and 【32†2】 and table 【33†1】).\nL79: Table 1 Features of the in silico metabolic model of R.eutropha H16\nL80: ## Methods\nL81: ### Reconstruction of the metabolic network\nL82: A metabolic network of R. eutropha H16 was constructed by combining information from various sources, such as public databases, literature, and experiments. The genome annotation information was obtained from NCBI 【34†http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】.\nL83: The biochemical reactions were collected and analyzed from various public databases, including Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG, [【35†8】]), BioSilico [【36†9】], Metacyc [【37†10】] and the University of Minnesota Biocatalyst/Biodegradation Database (UMBBD; 【38†http://umbbd.msi.umn.edu/†umbbd.msi.umn.edu】). The transport reactions were analyzed based on TransportDB [【39†11】] and literature [【24†1】, 【40†12】] as they are not sufficiently described in typical metabolic pathway databases.\nL84: The individual reactions were balanced with respect to element and charge for pH 6, 7, and 8. The charges of metabolites at different pH values were estimated based on their pK_{ a } values using the MarvinBeans software developed by ChemAxon 【41†http://www.chemaxon.com/download/†www.chemaxon.com】.\nL85: ### Fermentation\nL86: R. eutropha H16 (KCTC 22469; Korean Collection for Type Cultures, Daejeon, Korea) was used in this study. Cells were stored as a glycerol stock at -70°C before use. A seed culture was prepared in a 250 mL Erlenmeyer flask containing 100 mL of MR medium plus 20 g∙L^{-1} of D-fructose and cultured at 30°C and 250 rpm (JEIO Tech. Co. SI-900R). The MR medium contains per liter: (NH_{4})_{2}HPO_{4}, 4 g; KH_{2}PO_{4}, 6.67 g; citric acid, 0.8 g; MgSO_{4}∙3H_{2}O, 0.8 g, trace metal solution, 5 mL [【42†13】].\nL87: Batch culture was carried out in a 7.5 L Bioflo 310 fermentor (New Brunswick Scientific Co., Edison, NJ) containing 2 L of MR medium plus 20 g∙L^{-1} of D-fructose. Seed culture (200 mL) was used to inoculate the fermentor to give the initial OD_{600} of 0.5. For nitrogen-limited cultivation, (NH_{4})_{2}HPO_{4} was replaced with 4 g∙L^{-1} of Na_{2}HPO_{4} and 1.8 g∙L^{-1} of NH_{4}Cl.\nL88: Chemostat cultures were carried out at the dilution rates of 0.05, 0.07, and 0.10 h^{-1} by continuous feeding of the sterilized MR medium plus 20 g∙L^{-1} of D-fructose and simultaneous removal of culture broth using peristaltic pumps at the same rates (Cole-Parmer, Vernon Hills, IL). The steady states were determined by monitoring the constant concentrations of biomass, D-fructose, and organic acids in the fermentor for 5 consecutive samples taken at 1-3 h intervals.\nL89: The pH was controlled at 7.0 by automatic feeding of 2 M KOH. The dissolved oxygen concentration was maintained above 40% of air saturation by supplying air at 1vvm (air volume working volume^{-1} minute^{-1}) and by automatically controlling the agitation speed up to 1,000 rpm. Foaming was controlled by the addition of Antifoam 289 (Sigma, St. Louis, MO).\nL90: To investigate the effects of pH on PHB synthesis, cells were cultured in 250 mL Erlenmeyer flask containing 100 mL of nitrogen-limited MR medium plus 20 g∙L^{-1} of D-fructose at pH 6, 7, or 8 (30°C and 250 rpm). The pH was adjusted to 6, 7, or 8 with 2 M KOH solution.\nL91: ### Analytical procedures\nL92: The concentrations of D-fructose and organic acids were determined by high-performance liquid chromatography (Varian ProStar 210, Palo Alto, CA) equipped with UV/VIS (Varian ProStar 320, Palo Alto, CA) and RI (Shodex RI-71, Tokyo, Japan) detectors. A MetaCarb 87 H column (300 × 7.8 mm, Varian) was isocratically eluted with 0.01 N H_{2}SO_{4} at 60°C and a flow rate of 0.6 ML∙min^{-1}.\nL93: The OD_{600} was measured using an Ultrospec 3000 spectrophotometer (Pharmacia Biotech., Uppsala, Sweden) to monitor the cell concentration. Cell concentration defined as gram dry cell weight (gDCW) per liter of culture broth was calculated from the pre-determined standard curve relating the OD_{600} to DCW (1 OD_{600} = 0.448 gDCW∙L^{-1}) [【43†14】].\nL94: The content and monomer composition of the PHA were determined by gas chromatography (GC) [【44†15】]. Liquid cultures were centrifuged at 4,000 × g for 20 min, and then the cells were washed twice with distilled water, and dried overnight at 100°C. The dried cell pellet was subjected to methanolysis in the presence of 15% (v∙v^{-1}) sulfuric acid with benzoic acid as an internal standard.\nL95: The resulting methyl esters of 3-hydroxybutyrate (3HB) were assayed by GC according to the method reported previously [【44†15】]. GC analysis was performed by injecting 1 μL of sample into an Agilent 6890N GC system (Agilent Technologies, Palo Alto, CA) equipped with Agilent 7683 automatic injector, flame ionization detector, and a fused silica capillary column (ATTM-Wax; 30 m, ID of 0.53 mm, film thickness of 1.20 mm; Alltech, Deerfield, IL).\nL96: The GC oven temperature was initially maintained at 80°C for 5 min and ramped to 230°C at 7.5°C min^{-1}. It was then increased at a rate of 10°C min^{-1} until 260°C was reached and held for 5 min. Helium was used as a carrier gas. The injector and detector were maintained at 250 and 300°C, respectively. The PHB content (wt%) was defined as the percentage of PHB concentration (g∙L^{-1}) to cell concentration (g∙L^{-1}).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAcinetobacter baylyi Starvation-Induced Genes Identified through Incubation in Long-Term Stationary Phase - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2901722/)\nciteturn47view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2901722/\",\"pattern\":\"7.\"}); Total lines: 351\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Search\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Log in 【3†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【4†Image†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL5: \nL6:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL7:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL8:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL9:   * [Button: Log out ]\nL10: \nL11: Search… [Input: Search NCBI] Search NCBI\nL12: \nL13: Primary site navigation\nL14: \nL15: 【8†Image: Close†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL16: \nL17: Search [Input: Search NCBI]\nL18: \nL19: Logged in as:\nL20:   * 【5†Dashboard†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL21:   * 【6†Publications†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL22:   * 【7†Account settings†www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL23: \nL24: [Button: Log in ]\nL25: \nL26: 【1†Image: PMC search open icon†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 【8†Image: PMC search close ison†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL27: \nL28: Search PMC Full-Text Archive [Input: Search PMC Full-Text Archive] Search in PMC 【9†Image: Search†cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL29: \nL30:   * 【10†Journal List】\nL31:   * 【11†User Guide】\nL32: \nL33: [Button: Appl Environ Microbiol]\nL34: . 2010 May 28;76(14):4905–4908. doi: 【12†10.1128/AEM.01806-09†doi.org】\nL35: # Acinetobacter baylyi Starvation-Induced Genes Identified through Incubation in Long-Term Stationary Phase^{【13†▿】}^{ }^{【14†‡】}^{ }\nL36: \nL37: 【15†C Phoebe Lostroh†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL38: \nL39: ### C Phoebe Lostroh\nL40: \nL41: Department of Biology, Colorado College, Colorado Springs, Colorado 80903,^{1} Department of Biology, Grinnell College, Grinnell, Iowa 50112^{2}\nL42: \nL43: Find articles by 【15†C Phoebe Lostroh†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL44: \nL45: ^{1}, 【16†Bruce A Voyles†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL46: ### Bruce A Voyles\nL47: \nL48: Department of Biology, Colorado College, Colorado Springs, Colorado 80903,^{1} Department of Biology, Grinnell College, Grinnell, Iowa 50112^{2}\nL49: \nL50: Find articles by 【16†Bruce A Voyles†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL51: \nL52: ^{2,}^{*}\nL53: \nL54:   * [Button: Author information]\nL55:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL56:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL57: \nL58: Department of Biology, Colorado College, Colorado Springs, Colorado 80903,^{1} Department of Biology, Grinnell College, Grinnell, Iowa 50112^{2}\nL59: \nL60: ^{*}\nL61: Corresponding author. Mailing address: Department of Biology, Grinnell College, Grinnell, IA 50112. Phone: (641) 269-3038. Fax: (641) 269-4285. E-mail: voyles@grinnell.edu\nL62: \nL63: Received 2009 Jul 28; Accepted 2010 May 20; Issue date 2010 Jul.\nL64: \nL65: Copyright © 2010, American Society for Microbiology\nL66: \nL67: 【17†PMC Copyright notice】\nL68: \nL69: PMCID: PMC2901722  PMID: 【18†20511417†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL70: ## Abstract\nL71: Acinetobacter species encounter cycles of feast and famine in nature. We show that populations of Acinetobacter baylyi strain ADP1 remain dynamic for 6 weeks in batch culture. We created a library of lacZ reporters inserted into SalI sites in the genome and then isolated 30 genes with lacZ insertions whose expression was induced by starvation during long-term stationary phase compared with their expression during exponential growth.\nL72: The genes encode metabolic, gene expression, DNA maintenance, envelope, and conserved hypothetical proteins.\nL73: * * *\nL74: \nL75: Acinetobacter species are ubiquitous soil organisms. Starvation during long-term stationary phase (LTSP) can serve as a laboratory model for natural competitive conditions such as those found in soils (【19†4】). This model has been used to study Escherichia coli, and here, we have applied it to Acinetobacter baylyi strain ADP1 (【20†8】).\nL76: During long-term batch culture, an initially clonal population of Escherichia coli experiences five growth stages: lag, exponential, and stationary phases and then death phase and LTSP (【19†4】). Prior to LTSP, most of the cells die and serve as nutrition for starving survivors (【21†6】, 【22†13】). In LTSP, the cell population remains almost steady, declining slowly over years (reviewed in reference 【19†4】): for each newly dead cell, slightly less than one new cell is “born.”\nL77: Much of what is known about starvation physiology during LTSP has been determined through study of the growth advantage in stationary phase (GASP) phenotype. The phenotype arises from genetic changes that occur when cells experience LTSP. During LTSP, the population may have a mutation frequency approaching 1 in 600 base pairs per genome (【23†5】).\nL78: Some physiological changes that take place during LTSP have been described, as have some genes necessary for the development of GASP (【22†13】, reviewed in reference 【24†12】). Some mutant strains that exhibit GASP have mutations that enhance catabolic efficiency for processing amino acids (【25†14】-【26†16】). Another nutrient consumed is DNA, which requires genes homologous to strain ADP1's competence genes (【21†6】).\nL79: Additionally, mutations that knock out SOS polymerases interfere with the formation of GASP mutants (【27†11】).\nL80: ## Growth of Acinetobacter baylyi.\nL81: \nL82: One of our aims was to ascertain whether the phenomenon of LTSP occurs in A. baylyi. We cultured A. baylyi in aerated minimal succinate (0.01 M) broth (【28†7】) for 6 weeks at 37°C and measured the CFU ml^{−1} over that period (Fig. 【29†1】). Death phase began after about 24 h postinoculation, while LTSP began at 48 h postinoculation and persisted for at least five more weeks.\nL83: ### FIG. 1.\nL84: \nL85: 【30†Open in a new tab】\nL86: \nL87: Growth of Acinetobacter baylyi ADP1 in minimal medium with 0.01 M succinate (【28†7】) over 6 weeks. Data are from a sample representative of three trials.\nL88: ## Identification of starvation-induced genes.\nL89: Our second aim was to identify ADP1 genes induced by starvation during LTSP. To do this, we followed the method of Chakravorty et al. (【31†2】), digesting ADP1 chromosomal DNA with SalI and ligating the DNA to a lacZ-kan cassette from plasmid pKOK6, resulting in circular DNA that could enter the ADP1 genome by a single-crossover event following natural transformation (see the supplemental material).\nL90: We used a scaled-down version of the Miller method (【32†9】) in 96-well plates to screen 3,569 kanamycin-resistant colonies of ADP1 from six different ligation pools for mutants with higher levels of β-galactosidase activity during LTSP (48 h after inoculation) than during exponential phase (6 h after inoculation). There are 305 SalI sites in each genome (【33†1】), and most of the lacZ insertions were isolated from more than one independent pool, so the screen is near saturation.\nL91: One hundred twenty-three isolates were subjected to secondary screening and exhibited greater induction of β-galactosidase activity at 7 days postinoculation than during exponential phase. Examination of the strains using inverse PCR based on the sequence of the kanamycin cassette revealed 30 unique starvation-induced (STI) lacZ insertion sites (see the supplemental material).\nL92: The procedure used to introduce the lacZ-kan cassette into each strain should have resulted in merodiploid DNA caused by a single recombination event between the chromosome and a closed circle of DNA created by ligating SalI chromosomal fragments to SalI-digested cassette DNA. Using PCR, we detected an intact target open reading frame (ORF) in every strain.\nL93: The insertion of the cassette may have resulted in chromosomal rearrangements, so we also investigated the chromosome structure surrounding the cassette. We used PCR to attempt to amplify the sequence between the DNA adjacent to the 5′ end of the lacZ-kan cassette and the lacZ gene and the sequence between the DNA adjacent to the 3′ end of the lacZ-kan cassette and the kan gene. Twenty-seven strains were merodiploids without any obvious nearby rearrangements.\nL94: Three strains (AGCV5, AGCV7, and AGCV11) did not produce the expected PCR products, suggesting that they may have chromosomal rearrangements.\nL95: ## Expression of long-term stationary-phase genes.\nL96: \nL97: All 30 STI genes were induced at 7 days postinoculation compared with their expression during exponential growth (Fig. 【34†2B】; also see Table 【35†2】). When we moved the kanamycin-linked lacZ reporters into a fresh ADP1 strain through natural transformation using boiled cell extracts as DNA donors and selection for kanamycin resistance, the reporter genes of all 30 new reporter strains were still induced in LTSP (data not shown).\nL98: ### FIG. 2.\nL99: \nL100: 【36†Open in a new tab】\nL101: (A) Growth of four representative lacZ reporter strains in minimal medium with 0.01 M succinate (【28†7】). Squares, ostA::lacZ strain; diamonds, pilU::lacZ strain; triangles, argS::lacZ strain; circles, tolB::lacZ strain. (B) Induction of β-galactosidase in four representative lacZ reporter strains expressed as Miller units (【32†9】) (filled symbols, solid lines, left y axis) or nMol o-nitrophenol (ONP) per 30 min of incubation per 10^{6} CFU (open symbols, dotted lines, right y axis).\nL102: Squares, ostA::lacZ strain; diamonds, pilU::lacZ strain; triangles, argS::lacZ strain; circles, tolB::lacZ strain. Old populations of cells are turbidometrically dominated by “dead” cells that are not transcriptionally active (detected using LIVE/DEAD BacLight bacterial viability kit from Molecular Probes [Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA], which detects membrane potential; data not shown), prompting the use of nMol ONP/30 min/10^{6} CFU as an alternative measure of β-galactosidase activity.\nL103: ### TABLE 2.\nL104: \nL105: Expression of STI gene-lacZ fusions after 7 days of incubation in batch culture\nL106: Strain  | Functional group  | Gene interrupted by lacZ fusion and/or its product or function  | LacZ activity【37†^{a}】 (SD)\nL107: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL108: AGCV19  | Envelope  | ostA  | 2,389 (890)\nL109: AGCV23  | Gene expression  | trxA【38†^{b}】  | 1,534 (222)\nL110: AGCV12  | DNA metabolism  | pilU  | 1,025 (388)\nL111: AGCV1  | Metabolism  | argS  | 1,014 (63)\nL112: AGCV28  | Anabolism  | gshA  | 808 (218)\nL113: AGCV7  | Anabolism  | leuA  | 718 (87)\nL114: AGCV18  | Anabolism【39†^{c}】  | kdsB  | 682 (54)\nL115: AGCV25  | Unknown  | ACIAD3229, CHP  | 652 (126)\nL116: AGCV13  | Metabolism  | purA  | 608 (271)\nL117: AGCV10  | Unknown  | ACIAD0865, CHP  | 556 (346)\nL118: AGCV26  | Unknown  | ACIAD3343, CHP  | 427 (54)\nL119: AGCV8  | Unknown  | ACIAD0615, CHP  | 417 (15)\nL120: AGCV21  | Unknown  | ACIAD2746 but reversed,【40†^{d}】 CHP  | 390 (93)\nL121: AGCV2  | Unknown  | ACIAD0167, CHP  | 367 (48)\nL122: AGCV4  | Gene expression  | infB  | 359 (54)\nL123: AGCV14  | DNA metabolism  | Putative methylase  | 282 (131)\nL124: AGCV11  | Gene expression  | fusA  | 255 (132)\nL125: AGCV15  | Catabolism  | mdcA  | 233 (131)\nL126: AGCV5  | Envelope  | phaAB  | 225 (29)\nL127: AGCV20  | Envelope  | tolB  | 224 (15)\nL128: AGCV29  | Envelope  | Putative outer membrane protein  | 224 (15)\nL129: AGCV22  | Gene expression  | Putative RNA-binding protein  | 207 (62)\nL130: AGCV27  | Anabolism  | aroK  | 173 (25)\nL131: AGCV9  | DNA metabolism  | Putative mutT  | 157 (25)\nL132: AGCV3  | Catabolism  | atpA  | 133 (34)\nL133: AGCV17  | Unknown【38†^{b}】  | ACIAD1960, CHP  | 92 (21)\nL134: AGCV24  | Catabolism  | almA  | 56 (3)\nL135: AGCV16  | Catabolism  | Putative enoyl-coenzyme A hydratase  | 50 (14)\nL136: AGCV6  | DNA metabolism  | recD  | 39 (14)\nL137: AGCV30  | Gene expression  | 23S rRNA  | 11 (3)\nL138: 【41†Open in a new tab】\nL139: \nL140: ^{a}\nL141: \nL142: nmol o-nitrophenol/30 min/10^{6} CFU; see the supplemental material.\nL143: \nL144: ^{b}\nL145: \nL146: lacZ fusion is in the same orientation as trxA but is inserted after the stop codon, before the next gene (rho).\nL147: \nL148: ^{c}\nL149: \nL150: Surrounding genes that could be part of an operon with kdsB or ACIAD1960 suggest a role in transport.\nL151: \nL152: ^{d}\nL153: \nL154: lacZ gene is inserted opposite the direction of the open reading frame.\nL155: To further increase confidence in the identification of STI genes, we measured the population growth of null mutants (【42†3】) lacking one of six STI genes (ACIAD0167, ACIAD0615, ACIAD1960, ACIAD3229, or ACIAD3343, encoding conserved hypothetical proteins [CHPs], or pilU) or a control gene also encoding a CHP (ACIAD2370) but adjacent to and carried on the opposite strand relative to one of the STI genes.\nL156: All of the strains grew normally in minimal broth Davis (BD, Franklin Lakes, NJ) supplemented with 0.01 M succinate for the first 48 h of incubation at 37°C with aeration (P > 0.1; unpaired, two-tailed Student's t test). All six strains except the ACIAD2370::kan strain, the negative control, showed a significant decrease in survival at 7 days (P < 0.05; data not shown).\nL157: ## Bioinformatic analysis of STI genes.\nL158: Twenty-eight of the STI lacZ reporters were inserted in an open reading frame (Table 【43†1】). Seven of these genes (23%) encode CHPs (【42†3】). Twenty other STI genes have annotations that rely on limited similarity, conserved amino acid motifs, or the function of homologous genes studied in a distant relative. The only STI gene in Acinetobacter with an experimentally determined phenotype is almA, which is required for the degradation of long-chain N-alkanes (【44†10】).\nL159: ### TABLE 1.\nL160: \nL161: Bacterial strains isolated in this study\nL162: Strain  | Gene interrupted by lacZ fusion and/or its product or function  | Locus tag (prefix, ACIAD)  | Start codon【45†^{a}】  | SalI【46†^{b}】\nL163: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL164: AGCV1  | argS (arginyl-tRNA synthetase【47†^{c}】  | 0164  | 164048  | 163045\nL165: AGCV2  | CHP【48†^{d}】  | 0167  | 166880  | 168135\nL166: AGCV3  | atpA; ATP synthase F1 α-subunit【47†^{c}】  | 0185  | 181338  | 181779\nL167: AGCV4  | infB; translation initiation factor IF-2【47†^{c}】  | 0369  | 360876  | 362625\nL168: AGCV5  | phaAB【49†^{e}】; pH adaption K efflux system【47†^{c}】  | 0376  | 367423  | 368026\nL169: AGCV6  | recD; helicase involved in DNA repair【47†^{c}】  | 0399  | 397904  | 398700\nL170: AGCV7  | leuA【49†^{e}】; 2-isopropylmalate synthase【47†^{c}】  | 0530  | 516254  | 515403\nL171: AGCV8  | CHP  | 0615  | 606821  | 606613\nL172: AGCV9  | Putative MutT/Nudix protein【50†^{f}】  | 0673  | 668885  | 668708\nL173: AGCV10  | CHP  | 0865  | 851697  | 852072\nL174: AGCV11  | fusA【49†^{e}】; protein chain elongation factor【47†^{c}】  | 0884  | 866071  | 867823\nL175: AGCV12  | PilU【47†^{c}】  | 0911  | 893365  | 892531\nL176: AGCV13  | furA; adenylosuccinate synthetase【47†^{c}】  | 1258  | 1256743  | 1256803\nL177: AGCV14  | Putative N6 adenine methylase【50†^{f}】  | 1268  | 1270305  | 1268926\nL178: AGCV15  | mdcA; malonate decarboxylase【47†^{c}】  | 1753  | 1759599  | 1759642\nL179: AGCV16  | Putative enoyl-coenzyme A hydratase【50†^{f}】  | 1829  | 1836465  | 1837011\nL180: AGCV17  | CHP  | 1960  | 1947934  | 1947270\nL181: AGCV18  | kdsB; lipopolysaccharide synthesis【47†^{c}】  | 2362  | 2321280  | 2320904\nL182: AGCV19  | ostA; organic solvent tolerance outer membrane protein【47†^{c}】  | 2371  | 2328576  | 2328693\nL183: AGCV20  | TolB; outer membrane protein【47†^{c}】  | 2622  | 2575393  | 2576592\nL184: AGCV21  | CHP but reversed【51†^{g}】  | 2746  | 2688538  | 2687944\nL185: AGCV22  | Putative RNA-binding protein【50†^{f}】  | 2855  | 2792708  | 2793034\nL186: AGCV23  | trxA【47†^{c}】^{,}【52†^{h}】  | 3037-3038  | 2967985  | 2968468\nL187: AGCV24  | almA; monooxygenase【53†^{i}】  | 3192  | 3121361  | 3121180\nL188: AGCV25  | CHP  | 3229  | 3142675  | 3142777\nL189: AGCV26  | CHP  | 3343  | 3248853  | 3248995\nL190: AGCV27  | AroK【47†^{c}】  | 3354  | 3262302  | 3262221\nL191: AGCV28  | gshA; γ-glutamate-cysteine ligase【47†^{c}】  | 3549  | 3475214  | 3474012\nL192: AGCV29  | Putative outer membrane protein【50†^{f}】  | 3675  | 3588360  | 3587641\nL193: AGCV30  | 23S rRNA【54†^{j}】  | Unknown  | Unknown  | Unknown\nL261:   * 4.Finkel, S. E. 2006. Long-term survival during stationary phase: evolution and the GASP phenotype. Nat. Rev. Microbiol. 4:113-120. [【70†DOI†doi.org】] [【71†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】] [【72†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】]\nL262:   * 5.Finkel, S. E., and R. Kolter. 1999. Evolution of microbial diversity during prolonged starvation. Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U. S. A. 96:4023-4027. [【73†DOI†doi.org】] [【74†PMC free article】] [【75†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】] [【76†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】]\nL263:   * 6.Finkel, S. E., and R. Kolter. 2001. DNA as a nutrient: novel role for bacterial competence gene homologs. J. Bacteriol. 183:6288-6293. [【77†DOI†doi.org】] [【78†PMC free article】] [【79†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】] [【80†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】]\nL264:   * 7.Gerischer, U., and L. N. Ornston. 1995. Spontan